  "worked_solution": "Let's reverse the limits of integration on the known result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{6}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{6}^{0}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can split up the given integral using the sum rule and factor out the constants. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{6}(5f(x)+1)\\,\\text{d}x & =5{\\int }_{0}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,+\\,{\\int }_{0}^{6}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5(-4)\\,+\\,x\\,{|}_{0}^{6} \\\\  & =-20\\,+\\,(6-0) \\\\  & =-20\\,+\\,6 \\\\  & =-14.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
